    term: 'First Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Industry</span> refers to the economic activity concerned with the processing of raw materials and the manufacture of goods. Industries form the backbone of a nation\'s economy, converting resources into usable products and generating employment, income, and technological development.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Industry</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">An <span class="learn-keyword">industry</span> is any organized economic activity concerned with the extraction, processing, or production of goods and services for human use. Industries are usually classified according to the stage of production they occupy in the economy.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Types of Industries</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Description</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Primary Industry</td><td>Directly extracts raw materials from nature</td><td>Farming, fishing, mining, lumbering</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Secondary Industry</td><td>Processes/manufactures raw materials into finished or semi-finished goods</td><td>Textile mills, breweries, cement factories, car assembly</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Tertiary Industry</td><td>Provides services that support production and distribution</td><td>Banking, transport, insurance, trade, education</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Quaternary Industry</td><td>Deals with information, research and knowledge-based services</td><td>ICT, research and development, consultancy, biotechnology</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Importance of Industries in Economic Development</h3>\n<ul class="learn-list">\n  <li>Creation of employment opportunities for the growing population</li>\n  <li>Generation of government revenue through taxes and export earnings</li>\n  <li>Diversification of the economy away from dependence on a single sector</li>\n  <li>Provision of raw materials and finished goods, reducing reliance on imports</li>\n  <li>Encouragement of infrastructural development (roads, power, water)</li>\n  <li>Promotion of technological advancement and skill acquisition</li>\n  <li>Improvement in the standard of living of the citizens</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Relationship Between the Industry Types</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">The four types of industries are interdependent. Primary industries supply raw materials to secondary industries; secondary industries produce goods that tertiary industries distribute and sell; quaternary industries provide the knowledge, data and innovation that improve efficiency across the other three sectors. A well-balanced economy needs strength across all four levels.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Examiners often present a scenario (e.g. "a company that mines iron ore") and ask you to classify it. Remember: extraction = primary, processing/manufacturing = secondary, selling/service = tertiary, and research/data = quaternary.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Industry is the organized production of goods and services, classified into primary, secondary, tertiary and quaternary types. Industries drive economic development through employment, revenue, diversification and improved living standards.\n</div>\n  ',
