    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Geographic Information System (GIS)</span> is one of the most important modern tools in Geography. It allows geographers to capture, store, analyze, and display data that is linked to specific locations on the Earth\'s surface.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of GIS</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">A <span class="learn-keyword">Geographic Information System (GIS)</span> is a computer-based system designed to capture, store, manipulate, analyze, manage, and present all types of spatial or geographical data. It links data to a map, integrating location information (where things are) with descriptive information (what things are like).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Why Geography Uses GIS</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Traditional maps are static, but GIS allows dynamic, layered analysis of geographic data. A GIS can overlay information such as population density, rainfall, roads, and land use on the same map to reveal patterns and relationships that would be difficult to see otherwise.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Importance of GIS</h3>\n<ul class="learn-list">\n  <li>Helps in urban and regional planning by showing land-use patterns</li>\n  <li>Assists in disaster management (flood mapping, erosion monitoring)</li>\n  <li>Supports agricultural planning through soil and climate data analysis</li>\n  <li>Aids in resource management and environmental monitoring</li>\n  <li>Improves navigation and transport route planning</li>\n  <li>Supports decision-making in government and business through spatial analysis</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Uses of GIS in Planning</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Planners use GIS to site schools, hospitals, and roads by analyzing population distribution and existing infrastructure. It also helps in tracking changes over time, such as deforestation or urban sprawl, by comparing maps from different periods.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Do not confuse GIS with GPS. GPS (Global Positioning System) is used to determine exact location/coordinates, while GIS is the broader system used to store, analyze, and display spatial data — GPS is often just one source of data feeding into a GIS.</strong>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> GIS is a computer-based system for capturing, storing, analyzing and presenting spatial data. It is vital in planning, disaster management, agriculture, and resource management because it reveals patterns not visible on ordinary maps.\n</div>\n  ',
