      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  A Geographic Information System is not just software — it is a combination of several essential components that work together. Understanding these components helps in appreciating how GIS functions as a complete system.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. The Five Main Components of GIS</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Description</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Hardware</td><td>Physical equipment used to run GIS software and store data</td><td>Computers, GPS devices, scanners, plotters, servers</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Software</td><td>Programs that provide tools for capturing, storing, analyzing and displaying data</td><td>ArcGIS, QGIS, Google Earth</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Data</td><td>Geographic and attribute information the system works with</td><td>Satellite images, maps, census records, coordinates</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>People</td><td>Skilled individuals who design, manage and use the GIS</td><td>GIS analysts, planners, technicians, surveyors</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Methods</td><td>Procedures and models used to analyze and interpret the data</td><td>Spatial analysis techniques, data models, workflows</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Function of Each Component</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>Hardware</strong> provides the physical platform (input, processing, storage, and output devices) on which GIS operations run. <strong>Software</strong> provides the tools for entering, manipulating, and analyzing spatial data. <strong>Data</strong> is the core content — without accurate, up-to-date data, GIS analysis is meaningless. <strong>People</strong> design the system, ensure the data is accurate, and interpret the results for real-world decisions. <strong>Methods</strong> are the well-designed plans and rules that guide how the data is used and combined.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Why All Five Components Are Necessary</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">If any one component is missing or weak, the GIS cannot function effectively. For example, powerful software is useless without accurate data, and accurate data is useless without skilled people to interpret it correctly, following sound methods.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> A common WAEC-style question asks you to match a component to an example (e.g., "a GPS receiver" = Hardware; "ArcGIS software" = Software). Learn the five components with the mnemonic: Houses Solve Data People\'s Methods (Hardware, Software, Data, People, Methods).\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> GIS is made up of five interdependent components — Hardware, Software, Data, People, and Methods. Each plays a unique role, and all five must work together for GIS to function effectively.\n</div>\n  ',
