    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  The Earth\'s surface is constantly being shaped by <span class="learn-keyword">external (exogenetic) processes</span> — weathering, erosion, transportation, and deposition — which wear down high land and build up new landforms elsewhere.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Weathering</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Weathering</span> is the breakdown or decay of rocks in situ (in their original position) due to exposure to atmospheric agents. It does not involve movement of the broken material.</p>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Process</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Mechanical (Physical)</td><td>Rocks broken by physical forces without chemical change</td><td>Frost shattering, exfoliation from temperature change</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Chemical</td><td>Rocks decomposed through chemical reactions</td><td>Oxidation, carbonation, hydrolysis</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Biological</td><td>Rocks broken down by the action of living organisms</td><td>Plant roots, burrowing animals, lichen acids</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Erosion</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Erosion</span> is the wearing away and removal of rock material by natural agents such as running water, wind, ice (glaciers), and waves. Unlike weathering, erosion involves both breakdown AND movement of material.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Transportation</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Transportation</span> is the movement of eroded material from one place to another by agents like rivers, wind, glaciers, and waves, through processes such as traction, saltation, suspension, and solution.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Deposition</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Deposition</span> occurs when transporting agents lose energy and drop the material they are carrying, building up new landforms.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Resulting Landforms</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Landform</th><th>Formed By</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Valleys</td><td>River erosion (vertical/lateral)</td><td>Long depressions carved by flowing water</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Deltas</td><td>Deposition at river mouths</td><td>Fan-shaped land formed where a river meets a sea/lake and drops sediment</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Beaches</td><td>Deposition of sand/shingle by waves</td><td>Gently sloping accumulation of material along a coastline</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Dunes</td><td>Deposition of sand by wind</td><td>Mounds/ridges of windblown sand, common in deserts and coasts</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Floodplains</td><td>Deposition by rivers during flooding</td><td>Flat land bordering a river, built up from repeated flood deposits</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember the sequence: Weathering (breakdown in place) → Erosion (wearing away + removal) → Transportation (movement) → Deposition (dropping of material to form new landforms). Exam questions often ask you to identify which process produced a named landform (e.g., "what process forms a delta?" = deposition).\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> External processes — weathering, erosion, transportation and deposition — continually reshape the Earth\'s surface, producing landforms such as valleys (erosion) and deltas, beaches, dunes and floodplains (deposition).\n</div>\n  ',
