      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Building on earlier studies of weather, this topic examines <span class="learn-keyword">climate</span> — the long-term pattern of atmospheric conditions in a region — and the factors that control it across the globe.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Weather vs Climate</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Weather</th><th>Climate</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Time span</td><td>Short-term (daily/hourly)</td><td>Long-term (averaged over 30+ years)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Variability</td><td>Highly variable, changes often</td><td>Relatively stable pattern</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Example</td><td>"It is raining today"</td><td>"This region has a tropical climate"</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Elements of Climate</h3>\n<ul class="learn-list">\n  <li><strong>Temperature</strong> — degree of hotness or coldness of the atmosphere</li>\n  <li><strong>Rainfall/Precipitation</strong> — amount and distribution of moisture falling from the atmosphere</li>\n  <li><strong>Humidity</strong> — amount of water vapour present in the air</li>\n  <li><strong>Pressure</strong> — the weight of the atmosphere at a given point</li>\n  <li><strong>Wind</strong> — movement of air from high to low pressure areas</li>\n  <li><strong>Sunshine</strong> — duration and intensity of sunlight received</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Climatic Controls (Factors Affecting Climate)</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Latitude</td><td>Determines the angle of the sun\'s rays; areas near the Equator receive more direct heat than areas near the Poles</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Altitude</td><td>Temperature generally decreases with increasing height above sea level</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Distance from the sea</td><td>Coastal areas have more moderate climates; interior areas experience greater extremes (continentality)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Ocean currents</td><td>Warm currents raise coastal temperatures; cold currents lower them</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Prevailing winds</td><td>Winds from the sea bring moisture; winds from the land are usually dry</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Relief (mountains)</td><td>Mountains force air to rise, causing rainfall on windward sides and dryness on leeward sides (rain shadow)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Why Understanding Climate Matters</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Climate influences agriculture, water resources, settlement patterns, and the natural vegetation of a region. Understanding climatic controls helps explain why regions at the same latitude can still have very different climates.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> A frequent WAEC question asks students to distinguish weather from climate, or to explain why a coastal city differs in climate from an inland city at the same latitude. Focus your answer on continentality and ocean influence.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Climate is the long-term average of atmospheric conditions, described through elements like temperature, rainfall, humidity, pressure, wind and sunshine, and shaped by controls such as latitude, altitude, distance from the sea, ocean currents, winds, and relief.\n</div>\n  ',
