    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Because climates vary greatly across the world, geographers have developed systems to group similar climates together. The most widely used system is <span class="learn-keyword">Koppen\'s Classification</span>.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Koppen\'s Classification of Climate</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Wladimir Koppen developed a system that classifies world climates into major groups based mainly on temperature and precipitation patterns, closely linked to natural vegetation.</p>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Name</th><th>Key Feature</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>A</td><td>Tropical</td><td>High temperatures all year; no winter; heavy rainfall (e.g., Tropical Rainforest, Savanna)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>B</td><td>Dry (Arid/Semi-arid)</td><td>Low, unreliable rainfall; evaporation exceeds precipitation (e.g., Desert, Steppe)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>C</td><td>Temperate (Mild mid-latitude)</td><td>Mild winters, warm summers (e.g., Mediterranean, Humid subtropical)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>D</td><td>Continental</td><td>Cold winters, warm-to-hot summers, large temperature range</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>E</td><td>Polar</td><td>Very low temperatures all year; little precipitation (Tundra, Ice cap)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Other Approaches to Classifying Climate</h3>\n<ul class="learn-list">\n  <li><strong>Genetic classification</strong> — groups climates according to the causes (e.g., air masses, pressure systems)</li>\n  <li><strong>Empirical/statistical classification</strong> — groups climates based on observed data such as temperature and rainfall averages, like Koppen\'s</li>\n  <li><strong>Applied classification</strong> — groups climates based on their suitability for a specific purpose, e.g., agriculture or human comfort</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. World Climatic Regions</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Broadly, the world\'s climate can be grouped into: Equatorial/Tropical Rainforest, Tropical Monsoon, Savanna, Hot Desert, Mediterranean, Warm Temperate, Cool Temperate (Continental), Tundra, and Polar regions. Each region has characteristic temperature, rainfall, and vegetation patterns.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Importance of Climatic Classification</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Classifying climates helps geographers compare regions, understand agricultural potential, predict natural vegetation, and plan for human settlement and development.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Learn the five main Koppen groups by their letters (A, B, C, D, E) and one key defining feature for each — this is frequently tested directly.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Koppen\'s classification groups world climates into five major types (Tropical, Dry, Temperate, Continental, Polar) based on temperature and rainfall. Other approaches include genetic, empirical, and applied classifications, all helping geographers understand and compare world climatic regions.\n</div>\n  ',
