    term: 'First Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Environmental resources</span> are materials or components of the natural environment that are useful to humans. Understanding their types and how to use them sustainably is central to environmental geography.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Environmental Resources</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Environmental resources are elements of nature — such as water, soil, minerals, forests, and air — that have value to humans because they can be used to satisfy human needs and support economic activities.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Types of Environmental Resources</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Description</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Renewable Resources</td><td>Can replenish themselves naturally over a relatively short time if managed sustainably</td><td>Sunlight, wind, water, forests, fish stocks</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Non-renewable Resources</td><td>Exist in finite/fixed quantities and take millions of years to form; cannot be replaced once used up</td><td>Crude oil, coal, natural gas, minerals like gold and iron ore</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Importance of Environmental Resources</h3>\n<ul class="learn-list">\n  <li>Provide raw materials for industries (timber, minerals, crude oil)</li>\n  <li>Support agriculture through fertile soil and water resources</li>\n  <li>Provide energy for domestic, industrial, and transport use</li>\n  <li>Support biodiversity and ecological balance</li>\n  <li>Generate income and employment through resource-based industries</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Sustainable Use of Environmental Resources</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Sustainable use</span> means utilizing resources at a rate that does not deplete them faster than they can be replenished, ensuring they remain available for future generations. This includes practices like controlled logging, crop rotation, recycling, and the use of renewable energy sources.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Be able to classify a given resource as renewable or non-renewable and justify your answer. Also be ready to suggest sustainable practices for a named resource (e.g., "how can forest resources be used sustainably?" — answer with afforestation, controlled logging, etc.)\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Environmental resources are natural elements useful to humans, classified as renewable (can replenish, e.g., forests, water) or non-renewable (finite, e.g., crude oil, minerals). Sustainable use ensures these resources remain available for future generations.\n</div>\n  ',
