    term: 'Second Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  This topic deepens our understanding of <span class="learn-keyword">renewable</span> and <span class="learn-keyword">non-renewable resources</span>, examining their differences in more detail and their significance to Nigeria\'s economy.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning and Examples</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>Renewable resources</strong> can be naturally replenished within a relatively short period (e.g., sunlight, wind, water, forests, soil). <strong>Non-renewable resources</strong> exist in fixed quantities formed over millions of years and cannot be replaced once exhausted (e.g., crude oil, natural gas, coal, and solid minerals like tin and gold).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Key Differences</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Renewable</th><th>Non-Renewable</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Formation time</td><td>Short (days to a few years)</td><td>Millions of years</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Supply</td><td>Continuous if well managed</td><td>Fixed and depletable</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Environmental impact</td><td>Generally lower</td><td>Often higher (pollution, habitat destruction)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Examples</td><td>Solar, wind, water, forests, soil</td><td>Crude oil, coal, natural gas, minerals</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Significance to Nigeria\'s Economy</h3>\n<ul class="learn-list">\n  <li>Crude oil (non-renewable) remains Nigeria\'s major source of foreign exchange earnings</li>\n  <li>Solid minerals such as tin, coal, and limestone (non-renewable) support local industry</li>\n  <li>Agricultural land and forests (renewable) support food production, timber, and export crops</li>\n  <li>Rivers and hydro resources (renewable) support electricity generation (e.g., Kainji Dam)</li>\n  <li>Nigeria\'s over-dependence on crude oil (non-renewable) makes diversification into renewable-based sectors important for long-term stability</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Balancing Both Resource Types</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">A sound economy uses non-renewable resources wisely while investing revenue into developing renewable resource sectors (agriculture, forestry, solar/wind energy) to ensure long-term sustainability after non-renewable resources are exhausted.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Be ready to explain why Nigeria\'s heavy reliance on crude oil (a non-renewable resource) is risky for long-term economic planning, and to suggest renewable alternatives.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Renewable resources regenerate naturally within a short time, while non-renewable resources are finite and take millions of years to form. Nigeria\'s economy depends heavily on non-renewable crude oil, making the development of renewable resource sectors important for sustainable growth.\n</div>\n  ',
