    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Agriculture</span> is the deliberate cultivation of crops and rearing of livestock. It remains one of the most important economic activities in Nigeria, employing a large proportion of the population.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Types of Agriculture</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Description</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Subsistence farming</td><td>Farming mainly to feed the farmer\'s own family, with little or no surplus for sale</td><td>Small-scale food crop farming in villages</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Commercial farming</td><td>Large-scale farming carried out mainly for sale/profit</td><td>Large plantations of oil palm, rubber, cocoa</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Mixed farming</td><td>Combination of crop cultivation and livestock rearing on the same farm</td><td>A farm growing maize while also rearing cattle or poultry</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Importance of Agriculture</h3>\n<ul class="learn-list">\n  <li>Provides food for the population, ensuring food security</li>\n  <li>Supplies raw materials to agro-based industries (cotton for textiles, cocoa for beverages)</li>\n  <li>Generates employment for a large portion of the rural population</li>\n  <li>Earns foreign exchange through export of cash crops</li>\n  <li>Contributes to Gross Domestic Product (GDP)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Problems of Agriculture in Nigeria</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Explanation</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Land tenure system</td><td>Traditional land ownership practices limit access to large farmland for modern mechanized farming</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Poor storage facilities</td><td>Leads to high post-harvest losses of produce</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Inadequate funding/credit</td><td>Farmers struggle to access loans to buy inputs and equipment</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Poor rural infrastructure</td><td>Bad roads make it difficult to transport produce to markets</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Climate change and pests/diseases</td><td>Unpredictable weather and pest outbreaks reduce yields</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Low level of mechanization</td><td>Reliance on traditional tools reduces productivity</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Solutions to Agricultural Problems in Nigeria</h3>\n<ul class="learn-list">\n  <li>Land reform policies to improve access to farmland</li>\n  <li>Construction of modern storage and processing facilities</li>\n  <li>Provision of affordable agricultural credit and subsidies</li>\n  <li>Improvement of rural roads and infrastructure</li>\n  <li>Promotion of mechanized farming and use of improved seed varieties</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Learn to distinguish subsistence, commercial and mixed farming with clear Nigerian examples, and be ready to state at least three problems AND matching solutions — WAEC often asks for both together.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Agriculture in Nigeria includes subsistence, commercial and mixed farming, and is vital for food security, raw materials, employment and foreign exchange. However, it faces problems such as land tenure issues, poor storage, and inadequate funding, which can be addressed through targeted reforms and infrastructure investment.\n</div>\n  ',
