    topic: 'Manufacturing Industries in Nigeria',
    topicCode: 'SS2-GEO-13',
    module: 'Economic Geography',
    term: 'Second Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Manufacturing industries</span> convert raw materials into finished or semi-finished goods. In Nigeria, manufacturing plays a key role in economic diversification, though the sector faces several challenges.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Types and Examples of Manufacturing Industries in Nigeria</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Agro-based industries</td><td>Textile mills, food and beverage processing, cotton ginneries</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Petrochemical industries</td><td>Oil refineries, plastic and fertilizer production</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Building material industries</td><td>Cement factories, brick and tile making</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Metal and engineering industries</td><td>Vehicle assembly plants, steel rolling mills</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Consumer goods industries</td><td>Soap, cosmetics, and pharmaceutical manufacturing</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Factors Influencing Industrial Location</h3>\n<ul class="learn-list">\n  <li><strong>Availability of raw materials</strong> — industries locate near their raw material sources to reduce transport costs</li>\n  <li><strong>Access to markets</strong> — closeness to large population centers ensures ready buyers</li>\n  <li><strong>Availability of power supply</strong> — reliable electricity is essential for manufacturing operations</li>\n  <li><strong>Availability of labor</strong> — skilled and unskilled workforce needed for production</li>\n  <li><strong>Transport and infrastructure</strong> — good roads, rail, and ports ease movement of goods</li>\n  <li><strong>Government policy</strong> — incentives, tax holidays and industrial zones can attract industries</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Contribution to GDP</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Manufacturing contributes to Nigeria\'s Gross Domestic Product (GDP) by adding value to raw materials, creating jobs, generating tax revenue, and reducing dependence on imported finished goods.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Problems of Industries in Nigeria</h3>\n<ul class="learn-list">\n  <li>Epileptic (unreliable) power supply increasing production costs</li>\n  <li>Poor transport infrastructure raising the cost of moving goods</li>\n  <li>Limited access to affordable credit/financing for expansion</li>\n  <li>Competition from cheaper imported goods</li>\n  <li>Inconsistent government policies affecting long-term investment planning</li>\n  <li>Insecurity in some regions discouraging investment</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Learn the six factors of industrial location (raw materials, market, power, labor, transport, government policy) — WAEC often asks you to explain why a specific industry is sited in a specific location using these factors.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Nigeria\'s manufacturing industries include agro-based, petrochemical, building material, metal/engineering, and consumer goods sectors. Their location depends on raw materials, market access, power, labor, transport and government policy, but problems like power shortages and poor infrastructure continue to hinder growth.\n</div>\n  ',
