    topic: 'Direction and Bearing',
    topicCode: 'SS2-GEO-15',
    module: 'Map Reading and Practical Geography',
    term: 'Third Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Direction</span> and <span class="learn-keyword">bearing</span> are essential tools for describing the position of one place relative to another, forming the foundation of navigation and map reading.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Direction</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Direction</span> refers to the position of one point relative to another, usually described using the cardinal points (North, South, East, West) or intermediate points (North-East, South-West, etc.).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. The Compass and Its Points</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">A <span class="learn-keyword">compass</span> is an instrument used to determine direction, with a magnetized needle that always points to magnetic North. The compass has 16 points: 4 cardinal points (N, S, E, W), 4 intercardinal points (NE, SE, SW, NW), and 8 further sub-points.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Meaning of Bearing</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Bearing</span> is the direction of one point from another measured in degrees, clockwise from North (0°/360°). Bearings are always expressed using three digits (e.g., 045°, 090°, 270°).</p>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Direction</th><th>Bearing</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>North</td><td>000° / 360°</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>North-East</td><td>045°</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>East</td><td>090°</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>South-East</td><td>135°</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>South</td><td>180°</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>South-West</td><td>225°</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>West</td><td>270°</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>North-West</td><td>315°</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Use of Compass and Bearings in Navigation and Map Reading</h3>\n<ul class="learn-list">\n  <li>Pilots and ship captains use bearings to plot precise courses</li>\n  <li>Surveyors use bearings to accurately measure and record land boundaries</li>\n  <li>Hikers and explorers use a compass with bearings to navigate unfamiliar terrain</li>\n  <li>On maps, bearing helps describe the exact direction from one feature to another</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Back Bearing</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">A <span class="learn-keyword">back bearing</span> is the bearing of a point measured from the opposite direction. It is calculated by adding 180° if the original bearing is less than 180°, or subtracting 180° if it is 180° or more.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Practice converting between compass directions and their three-digit bearings, and calculating back bearings — these are common WAEC map-reading questions.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Direction describes the position of one place relative to another, while bearing measures this in degrees clockwise from North using three digits. Compasses and bearings are essential tools for navigation, surveying, and map reading.\n</div>\n  ',
