    term: 'Third Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  The study of <span class="learn-keyword">population</span> examines how many people live in an area, how they are distributed, and the challenges that arise from population imbalances.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Population</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Population</span> refers to the total number of people living in a particular area at a given time.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Factors Influencing Population Distribution</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Climate</td><td>Favorable climates (moderate rainfall/temperature) attract dense settlement; extreme climates (deserts, polar) tend to be sparsely populated</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Relief</td><td>Flat, low-lying land is easier to settle and farm than steep, mountainous terrain</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Soil fertility</td><td>Fertile soils support agriculture, attracting dense population</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Water availability</td><td>Areas near rivers, lakes, and coasts tend to be densely populated</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Economic opportunities</td><td>Areas with industries, jobs, and trade attract migrants</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Political and social factors</td><td>Stable, peaceful areas attract settlement; conflict zones are avoided</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Population Structure</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Population structure</span> refers to the composition of a population by age and sex, often illustrated using a population pyramid. It reveals the proportion of young, working-age, and elderly people in a society.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Population Density and Growth</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Population density</span> is the number of people living per unit area (usually per square kilometer). <span class="learn-keyword">Population growth</span> refers to the increase in population over time, driven by birth rates, death rates, and migration.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Problems of Overpopulation and Underpopulation</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Condition</th><th>Problems</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Overpopulation</td><td>Overcrowding, unemployment, pressure on housing/infrastructure, food shortages, environmental degradation</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Underpopulation</td><td>Labor shortages, underutilized resources, low economic output, inadequate defense/manpower</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Be ready to list factors affecting population distribution AND explain problems of both overpopulation and underpopulation — WAEC frequently tests both sides of this contrast.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Population refers to the number of people in an area, whose distribution is shaped by climate, relief, soil, water, and economic factors. Population structure, density, and growth reveal important social and economic patterns, while both overpopulation and underpopulation present distinct challenges.\n</div>\n  ',
