    topic: 'Settlements',
    topicCode: 'SS2-GEO-18',
    module: 'Human Geography',
    term: 'Third Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  A <span class="learn-keyword">settlement</span> is any place where people live, ranging from a small isolated homestead to a large sprawling city. Understanding settlement types, functions, and their siting factors is central to human geography.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning and Types of Settlement</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Rural settlement</td><td>Small, scattered settlements mainly engaged in agriculture and primary activities; low population density</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Urban settlement</td><td>Large, densely populated settlements with diverse economic activities (trade, industry, services); e.g., cities and towns</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Functions of Settlements</h3>\n<ul class="learn-list">\n  <li><strong>Administrative function</strong> — seat of government (e.g., state capitals)</li>\n  <li><strong>Commercial function</strong> — center for trade and business</li>\n  <li><strong>Industrial function</strong> — location of manufacturing activities</li>\n  <li><strong>Educational function</strong> — home to schools and universities</li>\n  <li><strong>Religious function</strong> — center of worship and pilgrimage</li>\n  <li><strong>Residential function</strong> — primarily provides housing for people</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Factors Affecting the Location of Settlements</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Explanation</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Water supply</td><td>Settlements often locate near rivers, lakes, or springs for drinking water and irrigation</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Relief</td><td>Flat land is preferred for building and farming over steep slopes</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Soil fertility</td><td>Fertile land attracts agricultural settlements</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Defense</td><td>Historically, hilltops or river-bend sites offered natural protection</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Trade routes</td><td>Settlements grow at crossroads, river confluences, or ports where trade is convenient</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Availability of building materials</td><td>Access to wood, stone, or clay for construction influences early settlement location</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Settlement Patterns</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Settlements may be arranged in different patterns: <strong>nucleated</strong> (clustered closely together, often around a central feature like a market or water source), <strong>dispersed</strong> (scattered over a wide area, common in farming regions), or <strong>linear</strong> (stretched along a road, river, or coastline).</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Learn to link a settlement\'s function to real Nigerian examples (e.g., Abuja = administrative function; Lagos = commercial/industrial function) — WAEC often asks for examples.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Settlements are classified as rural or urban and serve various functions (administrative, commercial, industrial, educational, religious, residential). Their location is influenced by water supply, relief, soil, defense, trade routes, and building materials, and they may form nucleated, dispersed, or linear patterns.\n</div>\n  ',
