    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Geo-political issues</span> are problems that arise from the interaction between geography and politics, often involving conflict over territory, resources, or identity within and between nations.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Geo-political Issues</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Geo-political issues</span> refer to conflicts, disputes, or tensions that arise from the relationship between geographic factors (such as boundaries and resources) and political power or governance.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Examples of Geo-political Issues in Nigeria and Africa</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Issue</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Boundary disputes</td><td>Disagreements over the exact demarcation of state or national borders, e.g., the Bakassi Peninsula dispute between Nigeria and Cameroon</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Ethnic conflicts</td><td>Tensions and clashes between different ethnic groups over land, resources, or political representation</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Political instability</td><td>Coups, contested elections, and weak governance that disrupt national stability</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Resource-based conflicts</td><td>Disputes over control of valuable resources such as oil, land, or water</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Farmer-herder clashes</td><td>Conflicts between crop farmers and pastoralist herders over land use, common in Nigeria\'s Middle Belt</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Causes of Geo-political Issues</h3>\n<ul class="learn-list">\n  <li>Colonial boundary-drawing that ignored ethnic and cultural groupings</li>\n  <li>Competition over scarce resources (land, water, minerals)</li>\n  <li>Weak governance and institutions</li>\n  <li>Uneven distribution of development and resources across regions</li>\n  <li>Religious and cultural differences</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Effects on Development</h3>\n<ul class="learn-list">\n  <li>Displacement of people and loss of lives</li>\n  <li>Destruction of property, farmland, and infrastructure</li>\n  <li>Disruption of trade, agriculture, and economic activities</li>\n  <li>Discouragement of both local and foreign investment</li>\n  <li>Diversion of government resources from development to security</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Be ready to cite specific Nigerian/African examples (Bakassi Peninsula, farmer-herder clashes) and to link causes directly to their effects on development — WAEC values concrete, real examples in answers.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Geo-political issues include boundary disputes, ethnic conflicts, and political instability, seen in examples like the Bakassi Peninsula dispute and farmer-herder clashes. They arise from colonial legacies, resource competition, and weak governance, and significantly hinder development through displacement, destruction, and disrupted investment.\n</div>\n  ',
