    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Data is the lifeblood of any GIS. Understanding the types and sources of <span class="learn-keyword">GIS data</span> is essential for effectively using GIS in planning and development.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Types of GIS Data</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Description</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Spatial data</td><td>Describes the location and shape of geographic features (where things are)</td><td>Coordinates of a road, boundary of a farmland plot, points marking school locations</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Attribute data</td><td>Descriptive information about the geographic features (what things are like)</td><td>Population of a town, type of soil, name of a road, land-use classification</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Formats of Spatial Data</h3>\n<ul class="learn-list">\n  <li><strong>Vector data</strong> — represents features as points, lines, and polygons (e.g., a city as a point, a river as a line, a country as a polygon)</li>\n  <li><strong>Raster data</strong> — represents features as a grid of cells/pixels, commonly used for satellite images and aerial photographs</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Sources of GIS Data</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Satellite imagery</td><td>Images captured from orbiting satellites, showing land cover, vegetation, and other features</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Aerial photography</td><td>Photographs taken from aircraft, providing detailed imagery of the ground</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Field surveys</td><td>Data collected directly on the ground using instruments like GPS receivers and total stations</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Existing maps</td><td>Digitized versions of paper maps and topographic sheets</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Census and statistical records</td><td>Population, economic, and social data collected by government agencies</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Uses of GIS Data in Planning and Development</h3>\n<ul class="learn-list">\n  <li>Urban and regional planning, siting of infrastructure</li>\n  <li>Disaster risk assessment and emergency response planning</li>\n  <li>Agricultural planning and natural resource management</li>\n  <li>Monitoring environmental change over time</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Be able to distinguish spatial data (location/shape) from attribute data (descriptive info), and vector data (points/lines/polygons) from raster data (grid/pixels) — these distinctions are frequently tested.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> GIS data is broadly categorized into spatial data (location/shape) and attribute data (descriptive information), stored in vector or raster format. It comes from sources like satellite imagery, aerial photography, field surveys, existing maps, and census records, and is vital for planning and development.\n</div>\n  ',
