    topic: "Earth's Internal Processes",
    topicCode: 'SS2-GEO-22',
    module: 'Physical Geography',
    term: 'Third Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  While external processes shape the Earth\'s surface from above, <span class="learn-keyword">internal (endogenetic) processes</span> originate from within the Earth, producing dramatic landforms through vulcanicity, earthquakes, folding, and faulting.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Internal Processes</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Internal processes</span> are geological processes driven by forces within the Earth\'s interior (heat and pressure from the mantle and core) that build up and deform the Earth\'s crust, in contrast to external processes that wear it down.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Vulcanicity</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Vulcanicity</span> refers to the movement of molten rock (magma) from within the Earth to or towards the surface. When magma reaches the surface, it is called lava, and it can build up volcanic landforms such as volcanic cones and mountains.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Earthquakes</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Earthquakes</span> are sudden, violent shaking of the Earth\'s crust caused by the release of built-up stress along fault lines, often at plate boundaries. They are measured using a seismograph and rated on the Richter scale.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Folding</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Folding</span> occurs when horizontal compressional forces bend rock layers without breaking them, producing upfolds called <strong>anticlines</strong> and downfolds called <strong>synclines</strong>. Fold mountains, such as the Alps and Himalayas, form through this process.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Faulting</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Faulting</span> occurs when rocks break and are displaced along a fracture (fault line) due to tensional or compressional stress. Faulting can produce landforms such as <strong>rift valleys</strong> (formed when a block of land sinks between two parallel faults) and <strong>block mountains/horsts</strong> (formed when a block of land is uplifted between faults).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">6. Landforms Associated with Internal Processes</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Process</th><th>Resulting Landform</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Vulcanicity</td><td>Volcanic mountains/cones, crater lakes</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Folding</td><td>Fold mountains (anticlines/synclines)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Faulting</td><td>Rift valleys, block mountains (horsts)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Earthquakes</td><td>Ground fissures, fault scarps, and secondary effects like landslides and tsunamis</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Learn to distinguish anticline (upfold) from syncline (downfold), and rift valley (down-dropped block) from horst/block mountain (uplifted block) — these paired terms are commonly confused in exams.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Earth\'s internal processes — vulcanicity, earthquakes, folding, and faulting — are driven by forces within the Earth\'s interior. They produce dramatic landforms such as volcanic mountains, fold mountains, rift valleys, and block mountains, contrasting with the surface-wearing external processes.\n</div>\n  ',
